    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4be9ce96-727c-5658-b4bf-630170c780d0",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sin {2 t},\\,y=6 \\cos^{2} {t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2 \\cos {2 t},\\,y'(t)=- 12 \\sin {t} \\cos {t}=- 6 \\sin {2 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(- 6 \\sin {2 t}\\right)}{\\left(2 \\cos {2 t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- 3 \\tan {2 t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{12},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =- 3 \\tan {2 \\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =- 3 \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
