      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4 \\sin {2 t},\\,y=3 \\sin^{2} {t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=8 \\cos {2 t},\\,y'(t)=6 \\sin {t} \\cos {t}=3 \\sin {2 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(3 \\sin {2 t}\\right)}{\\left(8 \\cos {2 t}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\tan {2 t}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3 \\tan {2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
