      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186083243/plots/curve-fab8588d14a9cd7e023a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sin {t},\\,y=\\cos {t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}.$ Give an exact answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\cos {t},\\,y'(t)=- \\sin {t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(- \\sin {t}\\right)}{\\left(\\cos {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\tan {t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}} & =- \\tan {\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
