      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186083243/plots/curve-d9833b5e1bb7f16b3280.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=5 \\cos {t},\\,y=4 \\sin {2 t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=- 5 \\sin {t},\\,y'(t)=8 \\cos {2 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(8 \\cos {2 t}\\right)}{\\left(- 5 \\sin {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8 \\cos {2 t}}{5 \\sin {t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{4 \\pi}{3},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =- \\frac{8 \\cos {2 \\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)}}{5 \\sin {\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8 \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{5 \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
